{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","methodChoice":"EDF Specification v0.3 (EDF-2 depth), evidence collected: X4 only (1 of 2 allowed)","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2","E5","X4"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3","X4"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E6","X4","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6","E4"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E1","X4"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E5","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"premature","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E4","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"premature","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E6","X4"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"preferred","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6","X4"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"insufficient","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E1","E3","E6","X4"]}],"brief":"What is probably going on: the Analyzer B potassium flag looks like a single chance or sampling glitch, not a systematic fault. The repeat Level 2 passed, Level 1 and 3 and the Level 2 average are unchanged since the new reagent lot, Analyzer A re-runs of the held samples agreed within limits, and patient medians are stable. The Ward 6 high potassiums are split evenly between the two analyzers and coincide with the tube station outage and long transit times, so the tube outage is the more likely driver, though not proven. Staff QC records show no skipped runs.

How sure: moderately. The main gap is whether the 14:10 sample was a short aspiration. The vendor says that cannot be ruled out, and the event log cannot show it. I have not reviewed the other 186 flagged results in full.

What to sign off: do not recall about 2,900 results, do not widen the limits, and do not record a sampling cause as confirmed. Consider returning Analyzer B to service with extra Level 2 QC each shift for a week, and keep the held-result review open until it is complete. Pursue the tube outage for Ward 6, and collect the vendor's answer on partial aspiration."}
